 solution handoff, validate the requirements, scope, assumptions, dependencies, risks, provisioning, stakeholders, and timeline, and then turn that into an executable delivery plan. From there I drive solution design, workstream ownership, build, test, train, and production deployment, with governance and project health running across the lifecycle. My job isn’t to personally do every specialist task; it’s to make sure the right work gets done by the right people at the right time.”**\n\nThat is the mental reset.\n\nIt tells Andrew, immediately, that you understand the chair you’re sitting in. Then when the case gets technical, you can go as deep as necessary without accidentally redefining yourself as the developer, architect, WMS specialist, integration engineer, or data lead.\n\nAnd it gives you the clean operating chain we just built:\n\n**Sold handoff → Validate → Plan → Design → Build → Test → Train → Deploy**  \nwith **Governance + Health/Commercial** across the whole implementation lifecycle.\n\nThen the three words you keep hearing in your head are:\n\n**Lifecycle. Requirements. Scope.**\n\nThose three will pull you back into Principal Consultant language every time the conversation starts drifting into the weeds.\n\nThat’s a very strong place to stop this first session. We’ve basically taken twenty years of instinct and put a visible rail around it. memcite", "text_chars": 1567, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "073e2ab5-f9f4-4c24-893e-37155d501097", "role": "tool", "role_name": "bio", "created_at": 1789363318.2966375, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "assistant", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Model set context updated.", "text_chars": 26, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-th