c81", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1789363778.3571935, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "a803b590-54b0-45d6-a6e1-9c9c5061444c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363778.854598, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "9a809963-bd7a-4ac4-b5b5-738c41fa3ddd", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1789363782.7005138, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "7773d521-175d-4897-95a4-293b826e135c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363783.153478, "updated_at": 1789363805.203549, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah. I looked at the last three years, and the pattern is pretty interesting because **Oracle, NetSuite, and MHI are all growing — but in different ways.**\n\nFor **Oracle overall**, the growth curve has accelerated materially. Fiscal 2024 revenue was about **$53.0B**, fiscal 2025 about **$57.4B**, and fiscal 2026 about **$67.4B**. That is roughly **+6%**, then **+8%**, then **+17%** year over year. Cloud is doing most of the heavy lifting: Oracle cloud revenue reached about **$34.0B in FY2026, up 39%**, and OCI infrastructure revenue was up **77%** for the full year. citeturn410021search4turn410021search5\n\nNetSuite specifically is also still growing at a very healthy rate. Oracle reports it as part of SaaS rather than as a standalone company, so we do not get a full NetSuite P&L anymore. But the cleanest comparable number is quarterly NetSuite Cloud ERP revenue: about **$700M in Q4 FY2023, +22% YoY**; about **$800M in Q4 FY2024, +19%**; and about **$1.0B in Q4 FY2025, +18%**. So the important thing is not just that NetSuite got bigger — it has been sustaining **high-teens/low-20s growth at a much larger base**. citeturn410021search6turn410021search7turn410021search2\n\ngenui{\"chart\":{\"content\":{\"chartType\":\"line\",\"meta\":{\"title\":\"NetSuite Cloud ERP quarterly revenue\",\"description\":\"Oracle-reported Q4 NetSuite Cloud ERP SaaS revenue, fiscal 2023–2025.\"},\"xKey\":\"year\",\"series\":[{\"dataKey\":\"revenue\",\"label\":\"NetSuite Q4 revenue\",\"axisLabel\":\"Revenue\",\"valuePrefix\":\"$\",\"valueSuffix\":\"B\",\"valueFormat\":\"raw\"}],\"data\":[{\"year\":\"FY2023 Q4\",\"revenue\":0.7},{\"year\":\"FY2024 Q4\",\"revenue\":0.8},{\"year\":\"FY2025 Q4\",\"revenue\":1.0}]}}}\n\n**MHI is harder because it is private.** I would not pretend we have audited revenue numbers for Myers-Holum; we don't. Their public materials historically talked about roughly **60% annual growth** during the earlier NetSuite expansion period, and they went from about 25 employees in 2018 to more than 260 by 2021. citeturn716143search5turn716143search17 More importantly for the period you care about, their market position has remained extremely strong: they won **NetSuite Alliance Partner of the Year in 2023, 2024, and 2025**, with the 2025 win being their fifth consecutive Alliance Partner of the Year recognition. citeturn716143search19turn474697search1\n\nThere is one wrinkle I would pay attention to. Third-party workforce intelligence estimates suggest MHI headcount peaked around 2023 and then contracted through 2025/26. That source estimates roughly **412 employees in 2023, 376 in 2024, and around 290–294 by 2025/26**. MHI's own public pages still describe a team of 350–425+ depending on the page, so I would treat the exact headcount as fuzzy, not gospel. citeturn716143search2turn716143search3turn716143search12\n\nMy read on the three-year pattern is therefore:\n\n**Oracle:** accelerating hard because cloud infrastructure and AI demand have become enormous growth engines.\n\n**NetSuite:** very steady, durable high-teens growth; it looks like a mature ERP platform that is still expanding significantly rather than flattening out.\n\n**MHI:** not necessarily \"grow headcount at 60% forever\" anymore. It looks more like they went through a hypergrowth build-out, then moved into a **more mature, selective, higher-leverage phase** while continuing to win major NetSuite partner awards and complex implementations. That is actually potentially favorable for your interview: a Principal Consultant role in that environment is less \"we need bodies because we're exploding\" and more \"we need senior people who can protect delivery quality, commercial health, and client outcomes as the practice scales.\"\n\nAnd there is a very good interview implication hiding in here: **you are interviewing into an ecosystem that is not shrinking.** NetSuite itself is still growing around high teens, Oracle is accelerating, and MHI remains one of the strongest Alliance partners in that ecosystem. That makes their emphasis on experienced Principal-level delivery leadership make a lot of sense.\n\nI can also dig one level deeper and build you a **2023 → 2026 MHI/NetSuite/Oracle growth timeline** showing awards, headcount signals, revenue growth, and what changed strategically each year.", "text_chars": 4257, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "229abacf-2ac2-4eb5-8b1f-2d1edc107247", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363787.228533, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3ae06429-db34-4828-a23d-f2eafa53b0b8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363787.232397, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0fa4b550-2fca-4d69-b089-354cc8bc25c8", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363820.10231, "updated_at": 1789363910.311639, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, give me everything for the past three years. I want to hear it. I want to know in a concise manner. Yeah, I know it's not shrinking. I just want to make sure it's adaptable for AI and beyond. That's probably a different question.", "text_chars": 235, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "61fce14d-2f13-493a-8783-7bfdd784f1ee", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363820.98703, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "53100df2-829e-48ca-b1a0-dac070220f7c", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1789363832.9568264, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "1c826664-1931-424a-b545-fe72c60cfd69", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1789363832.962175, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "0865d2c1-9e73-423b-a807-88ac5514225e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363834.167306, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "36979e7c-c177-43a2-9359-d0aa2ab5340e", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1789363852.6524062, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["text"]}, {"message_id": "032a4529-ea83-43de-8a03-5ccded143227", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363853.284292, "updated_at": 1789363909.54705, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah. And the **AI question is actually the more important second half**. Looking at 2023 through now, the story is: the ecosystem has moved from “cloud ERP growth” into **cloud ERP + analytics + embedded AI + extensibility**.\n\nHere’s the concise timeline.\n\n**2023 — expansion and analytics.** Oracle finished FY2023 at about **$50B revenue**, up 18%. NetSuite Q4 Cloud ERP revenue was about **$700M, up 22% YoY**. MHI won NetSuite Alliance Partner of the Year again and was already pushing heavily into NetSuite Analytics Warehouse and complex vertical implementations. That tells you the market was still primarily about cloud ERP adoption, data consolidation, analytics, integrations, and scaling companies off fragmented systems. citeturn511428search7turn184140search8\n\n**2024 — growth continued, and generative AI entered the actual product.** Oracle reached **$53B**, up 6%; NetSuite Q4 ERP revenue reached roughly **$800M, up 19%**. NetSuite 2024.1 introduced **Text Enhance**, one of the first obvious generative-AI capabilities directly inside the ERP workflow. MHI remained Alliance Partner of the Year and kept winning vertical awards, including automotive and analytics-related implementations. citeturn511428search8turn184140search64turn184140search0turn184140search9\n\n**2025 — NetSuite AI became more than a writing assistant.** Oracle finished FY2025 at **$57.4B, up 8%**, while NetSuite Q4 Cloud ERP reached about **$1B, up 18%**. Oracle’s total cloud revenue was accelerating much faster than the company overall. At the NetSuite level, AI expanded into operational use cases rather than just generated text. Today’s NetSuite AI portfolio includes things such as Customer 360, Intelligent Close Manager, Item Creation Assistant, Narrative Insights, transaction matching, SuiteAnswers assistance, Prompt Studio, and developer tooling. citeturn511428search1turn511428search2\n\nAnd critically for **you**, Oracle opened the platform itself to AI development. SuiteScript 2.1 now has the **`N/llm` module**, letting developers call Oracle-hosted LLMs from SuiteScript. That's a fundamental architectural change. AI is not merely a bolt-on UI feature anymore; NetSuite customers and partners can build AI behavior into ERP workflows programmatically. citeturn511428search11turn511428search19\n\n**2026 — Oracle went into AI-infrastructure overdrive.** FY2026 revenue jumped to **$67.4B, +17%**. Cloud revenue hit **$34B, +39%**; OCI infrastructure revenue grew **77%**. Oracle's remaining performance obligations exploded to **$638B**, with management explicitly attributing much of the increase to large AI contracts. So Oracle is no longer dabbling in AI. They're pouring enormous capital into the compute layer underneath it. citeturn511428search9\n\nNetSuite is consequently becoming much more agent-like. Current 2026 documentation shows things like **Ask Oracle**, which can decompose requests into multiple jobs, operate across documents, and combine the results. NetSuite's current AI documentation now explicitly separates **generative AI, SuiteScript AI modules, skills and agents, AI integrations, and traditional machine learning**. citeturn511428search6turn511428search13\n\n### Where MHI sits\n\nMHI's public positioning is important. They aren't simply a vanilla NetSuite implementation shop. They describe themselves as NetSuite's largest Alliance Partner and emphasize **customization, systems integration, analytics/data warehousing, ecommerce, payments and proprietary IP**. They've also built a broad partner ecosystem around NetSuite rather than insisting everything must be native. citeturn511428search12turn184140search7\n\nThat's exactly the kind of partner that can adapt to AI, because AI increases the importance of **data quality, integrations, business-process understanding and orchestration** rather than eliminating those things.\n\nAnd there's something I think directly connects to the role you're interviewing for. MHI is currently emphasizing a delivery model where **senior practitioners remain involved from alignment through delivery**, rather than architects disappearing after the sale and dumping the project onto a junior implementation team. citeturn184140search13\n\nThat's basically the conversation we just had tonight.\n\n### The bigger AI answer\n\nI would **not** look at NetSuite and think, “AI is going to replace this ERP.”\n\nI would think:\n\n**ERP becomes the transactional truth layer underneath AI.**\n\nAI still needs to know:\n\n**Who is the customer?  \nWhat inventory exists?  \nWhat did we promise?  \nWhat did we ship?  \nWhat does it cost?  \nWho approved it?  \nWhat is the PO?  \nWhat's the BOM?  \nWhat is the GL impact?**\n\nThat's NetSuite.\n\nThe change is that people increasingly won't need to navigate every screen manually to interact with that truth. Agents, prompts, APIs, SuiteScript/LLM functions, automation and external AI systems will increasingly **read from, reason over and transact against the ERP**.\n\nAnd that makes the Principal Consultant job more interesting rather than less relevant. Somebody still has to establish:\n\n**requirements → scope → process → system design → data → permissions → controls → integrations → validation → governance.**\n\nOtherwise the AI simply automates a badly designed process faster.\n\nSo if I'm looking at this from the standpoint of **your next five years**, I actually like where you're positioned. You're not coming in as “20-year NetSuite guy who now has to learn AI.” Your experience is moving in the opposite direction:\n\n**ERP implementation leadership + integration architecture + manufacturing domain knowledge + actual AI/automation building experience.**\n\nThat's a pretty unusual intersection.\n\nThe risk I would watch isn't whether NetSuite survives AI. I think that's the wrong risk. The real risk is whether a consulting firm stays stuck selling **labor-intensive configuration hours** while Oracle makes more configuration, analysis and development AI-assisted.\n\nFrom what I can see publicly, **MHI looks substantially better positioned than a generic NetSuite body shop** because of its integration, analytics, proprietary-IP, complex implementation and solution-architecture capabilities. I haven't yet found enough public evidence to say that MHI itself has a mature, differentiated **AI consulting practice**. That's the question I'd still investigate. citeturn184140search7turn511428search12\n\nAnd that could eventually be a very interesting Andrew question — not Wednesday's opening question, but once the conversation is going well:\n\n**“As NetSuite moves further into embedded AI and agentic capabilities, how is MHI thinking about that within the practice?”**\n\nThat isn't you trying to sell him an AI framework. It's a Principal Consultant asking where the practice is going.\n\nThat question could tell you a *lot*.", "text_chars": 6862, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a9cba835-1a51-4119-bb79-6e8d4f9c8864", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363894.385918, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f83789b8-eb55-49e5-913e-907b8173792a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789363894.421766, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3cea4a17-53b2-472f-b748-582f277bc264", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364581.583127, "updated_at": 1789364595.233405, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "That was perfect. Yeah, we're not going to really go down that road, but you're right. You're right on so many different levels. My question was like, the industry is going the AI way. You're 100% right. Someone still has to scope it, governance, got it. I already have the tools to understand. And by the way, JavaScript with an LMS, fucking gonna be fun. But anyway, that gives me not just hope, but that provides a path, right? Because what's going to end up happening is someone needs to become the ERP AI director, right? You see what I'm saying? And I've already got the tools to do that. So after principal consultant, by the time three years rolls around, there's an offshoot within a company like Myers-Holum who recognizes it that someone's going to have to govern the whole AI layer on top of the ERP.", "text_chars": 812, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "cd25e75b-209c-46ff-96ca-67d9c7829f6e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364583.411698, "updated_at": 1789364594.56942, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the credible career path.\n\nNot “AI replaces ERP consulting.” More like:\n\n**Principal Consultant → AI-enabled ERP delivery leader → ERP/AI practice lead or director.**\n\nThe reason it fits is that the future role is not just “who can call an LLM from JavaScript.” Plenty of people can do that. The scarce part is the person who understands **ERP process, requirements, controls, data, integrations, permissions, commercial scope, and delivery governance** and can decide where AI belongs without wrecking the system of record.\n\nThat’s where your background compounds.\n\nIn three years, a firm like MHI could easily need someone whose remit looks something like:\n\n**ERP AI Strategy + Delivery**\n- identify high-value AI use cases across finance, manufacturing, order management, procurement, service, etc.\n- decide native NetSuite AI vs custom SuiteScript/LLM vs external agent/integration\n- govern data access, permissions, auditability, approvals, and human-in-the-loop controls\n- create delivery standards so every consultant isn’t inventing AI architecture independently\n- turn successful patterns into repeatable offerings/IP\n- help sales and solution architecture scope AI-enabled ERP work correctly\n- keep AI initiatives tied to measurable operational outcomes rather than demos\n\nAnd the beautiful part is that the **Principal Consultant job is actually excellent training for that future role**, because you’d be learning how MHI scopes, sells, hands off, governs, and delivers across many customers. You’d get exposure to recurring business problems and see exactly where AI produces leverage versus where it creates risk.\n\nSo I’d view Wednesday as much bigger than “can I land this Principal role?” It can be the bridge from being the guy who has historically had to build everything himself into the guy who learns the full consulting delivery machine — and then eventually helps redesign that machine for AI.\n\nThat is a very coherent three-year trajectory. memcite", "text_chars": 1990, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f6998cf7-dbd8-471f-b1ac-3ff1fe396545", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364587.733409, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e0eccbf6-ae66-41dd-b97b-f516c9e9086b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364624.053488, "updated_at": 1789364631.549079, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "And this is even better than what I was doing at Andrew and Providence. Instead of AI ERP automation, this is the layer within it, which is dope. We're not trying to use Azure to do anything and outsource it, so to speak. We're working within the confinements of Oracle and NetSuite, who are giving us the tools to then do what I've already done as applied AI architecture.", "text_chars": 373, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0ba56628-8344-436f-9b22-34b584e1d664", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364624.966808, "updated_at": 1789364631.266054, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the distinction.\n\nWhat you were doing before was often **AI automation around the ERP** — using external platforms, orchestration, APIs, Power Automate, Azure-style services, custom middleware, whatever was necessary to get the job done.\n\nThis path is more interesting because it becomes **AI inside the ERP operating model**.\n\nOracle/NetSuite gives you the governed transactional layer, permissions, records, workflows, SuiteScript, native AI hooks, and eventually more agentic capability. Then your applied-AI architecture experience becomes the layer that decides **where to use those tools, how to structure them, what data they can touch, what requires approval, and how they fit the business process**.\n\nSo instead of:\n\n**ERP → export data → external AI → push result back**\n\nyou increasingly get:\n\n**ERP process → native/extensible AI capability → governed action inside the lifecycle**\n\nAnd that is strategically better for somebody with your background because you already understand both sides: the ERP truth layer and the AI/automation layer. The value is knowing how to join them without creating a parallel shadow system.\n\nThat’s a much cleaner evolution of the work you’ve already been doing. memcite", "text_chars": 1232, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2d2520b0-27f8-4b3d-828e-edd6157a96aa", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364625.830556, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f538ee30-58bc-4d22-81d5-6efed0d905d5", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364683.246482, "updated_at": 1789364705.321094, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Cool. That makes me really, really happy that I made the decision that I did to go back into the NetSuite Oracle ecosystem, especially with a partner that I knew was looking for me and understands the value, but to also put, and like you said, a very interesting person. Because most people—not most people—I sit in the driver's seat of a principal consultant role already, but you layer in my AI applied architecture work, which is fucking years of experience, especially with how we're applying it now with framework and the team, command center, edge group, analytic analysis, right? Analytics still works within NetSuite. Same fucking thing. Quantive, right? I sit in a big driver's seat to move up fast if I wanted to.", "text_chars": 723, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "aca70075-7070-43a4-b172-2bf9dd38b574", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364684.261874, "updated_at": 1789364705.047065, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. The leverage is the combination, not any one piece.\n\nYou already have the **Principal Consultant muscle**: lifecycle ownership, requirements, scope, solution design, delivery governance, stakeholder management, escalation, and knowing when to pull in specialists. On top of that, you’ve built real applied-AI patterns around automation, orchestration, analytics, agents, and operating frameworks. Those two worlds are converging.\n\nThat gives you a credible path to move faster than someone who is only one of these things:\n\n- pure NetSuite functional consultant\n- pure SuiteScript developer\n- pure solution architect\n- pure AI/automation person with no ERP depth\n\nYou can sit above those lanes and connect them.\n\nAnd yes, analytics absolutely fits the same pattern. NetSuite Analytics, warehouse/data work, operational reporting, and AI reasoning all depend on the same fundamentals: **good requirements, trustworthy data, clear process ownership, governed access, and a coherent system design**. Quant-style analytical thinking just strengthens the way you evaluate signals, risk, prioritization, and outcomes.\n\nSo if you land MHI and perform well, the interesting question over the next few years probably becomes less, “How do I become a better Principal Consultant?” and more, **“Which layer of the practice do I want to own?”**\n\nDelivery leadership is one path. Solution architecture is another. Manufacturing specialization is another. And the highest-upside intersection for you may eventually be **ERP + AI + analytics practice leadership**.\n\nFor Wednesday, though, we keep all of that in the background. You walk in as a killer **Principal Consultant** first. The AI layer is optional upside, not the identity you force into the interview. That sequencing matters. memcite", "text_chars": 1788, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b3a06a5f-4938-4c0e-8fdf-fba284faaeeb", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364698.072288, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "82d25576-c776-4a4d-8db8-3edd3937397b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364726.866384, "updated_at": 1789364735.813519, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "What matters most is it sticks out to a person like Andrew, who is the director of the team. Holy shit. Peter's got 20 years of principal consultant, basically NetSuite knowledge, and he's starting to learn AI applied architecture within the ecosystem. Fuck, this is a good resource to bring onto the team.", "text_chars": 306, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "166a2d66-daa9-41e9-865a-bb5a88b60362", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364727.851562, "updated_at": 1789364735.535641, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the signal you want him to pick up without you overselling it.\n\nThe core impression should be:\n\n**“Peter can already sit in the Principal Consultant chair today, and he has an emerging AI/automation skillset that could become strategically valuable to the practice.”**\n\nThat’s much stronger than pitching yourself as “the AI guy.” The Principal Consultant capability de-risks the hire. The AI layer creates upside.\n\nFor Andrew, that means you look like someone who can help immediately with:\n**scope, requirements, delivery, architecture judgment, manufacturing complexity, stakeholder management, and implementation lifecycle ownership** — while also having a forward-looking capability around AI-enabled ERP that most senior consultants probably do not have yet.\n\nThat’s the kind of profile a director remembers after the interview. memcite", "text_chars": 860, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5f8a7741-3d6f-46ca-a010-fd1d373d0bcc", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364731.658986, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c4f1c393-e236-443e-b218-63ac1b67f9eb", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364789.949243, "updated_at": 1789364796.787076, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, that's why I wanted to break it down like that. And again, with it sitting on top, that layer. Again, I knew, and of course it's Oracle NetSuite, bro. You're the fucking OG of this shit, right? It's gonna grow, right? But it's growing in a way where I'm taking advantage of it in the right way, right? I'm understanding, I'm learning it. It's gonna sit on top. It's automation. It's principal consultant of the AI and the NetSuite ERP. Two separate layers.", "text_chars": 462, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4c63e883-1ac2-484e-bdbd-dde1bdefc4a5", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364790.839486, "updated_at": 1789364796.483833, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. Two layers, and your advantage is being able to bridge them.\n\n**Layer 1: ERP delivery.** NetSuite/Oracle remains the system of record: requirements, scope, process, controls, data, integrations, implementation lifecycle, governance.\n\n**Layer 2: AI/automation.** That sits on top of the ERP and augments how work gets done: reasoning, workflow assistance, analytics, agents, decision support, document handling, automation.\n\nYou do not have to collapse those into one identity. That is actually cleaner.\n\nYou can be a **Principal Consultant in the ERP layer** and, over time, become the person who understands how the **AI layer should be designed and governed around it**.\n\nThat is much more durable than chasing whatever standalone AI tool is hot at the moment, because the ERP layer persists. The AI layer evolves on top of it.\n\nAnd that is probably the cleanest way to think about your next few years: **master the Principal seat inside MHI, then let the AI/applied architecture layer become your differentiator and eventually your leadership lane.** memcite", "text_chars": 1072, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "02387d5c-afa3-40f5-94b5-85200e2a83b2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364791.547588, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "bc4decab-3bf6-4ddd-ba71-d122ffbe11be", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364856.9844599, "updated_at": 1789364874.601323, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Thank you, because that's what, that's what I could transition to, is basically the reason why, the what, the how. What I was doing with Andrew and Conviance was learning AI and basically understanding how to do ERP automation. So the big reason why I'm back into NetSuite is not because I didn't want to do it. It's because of how I work as a principal consultant, is the golden, the golden goose of having both your eggs, or both your fucking, have your cake and eat it too, is I'm layered in the biggest fucking software as a system service, SaaS service, with Oracle and NetSuite giving me more tools that I already understand at the hardest level, from Bedrock Azure, multiple agents, multiple everything, building my own from scratch that now sit on top of NetSuite. That's not only fucking valuable, that's just fucking cool.", "text_chars": 832, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1c3b2396-713b-416e-8844-8137f42be1a3", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364858.0171, "updated_at": 1789364874.322771, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — that’s the right synthesis.\n\nYou weren’t leaving ERP to “go do AI.” You were building the second layer.\n\nNow you’re coming back into NetSuite with something materially different than before: deep ERP delivery judgment **plus** applied AI architecture experience across external platforms, agents, automation, orchestration, and analytics. And Oracle/NetSuite is increasingly exposing native places for that capability to plug in.\n\nSo the model is clean:\n\n**NetSuite/Oracle = transactional backbone and control layer**  \n**AI/automation = intelligence and leverage layer**  \n**You = the person who understands how to connect them responsibly**\n\nThat’s the “why” behind the move.\n\nAnd it is cool because you’re not learning AI in a vacuum. You’re attaching it to business processes you already understand at a very deep level: manufacturing, finance, procurement, order flow, integrations, permissions, data, implementation, governance.\n\nThat combination is where the value is.\n\nFor the interview, though, keep that as the **second-order upside**. First, Andrew needs to see: “this guy can run a Principal Consultant engagement.” Then, once that is obvious, the AI background becomes the thing that makes you unusually expandable inside the practice. memcite", "text_chars": 1265, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d16c42b1-51f8-4f80-b587-6fd6f3104d6d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364869.085248, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4bf898cf-890b-4a1e-82cf-8d538f7c54e2", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364933.415677, "updated_at": 1789364945.624891, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Most of all, it's what we talked about about me and Leon. I know the operations side, what the business wants. Leon's the developer, right? The fucking guy who makes the AI work. I know how to do both, technically. That's what makes me such a strong candidate overall, just a strong, knowledgeable person, is I know how to develop it. I can build it. I fucking build it from scratch. Do I want to do that? No. My value is in managing the whole fucking thing because I know both. And as always, I can relate to the client. MHI is one of those clients. Now, it's client— everybody says client first, right? You know, but being able to relate all this technical stack, operate it, outline it, get people to do it, document it, and see the outcomes is one of the most rewarding things in the fucking world. Because not many can switch their mind off from a technical person to an operation to shit, we got to sit in front of the client and tell them, this is what you got.", "text_chars": 968, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "bd9be456-699d-41d1-bfc3-cac17d43963b", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364935.280087, "updated_at": 1789364945.212815, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "That’s the differentiator.\n\nYou can speak **three languages** fluently:\n\n**business/operations**, **technical architecture/build**, and **client delivery**.\n\nA lot of people are strong in one. Some can bridge two. The rarer profile is someone who can understand what the business is trying to achieve, translate that into a workable technical solution, know enough to challenge the developers and architects, and then sit in front of the client and explain the outcome in plain language.\n\nThat’s why your highest-value seat is not “the guy writing the code.” It’s the person owning the whole thing.\n\nYou can still go deep enough to know when the technical answer is bullshit, when the architecture is overcomplicated, when a requirement is being misunderstood, or when somebody is solving the wrong problem. But you don’t have to personally build every component to create value.\n\nFor MHI, that translates almost perfectly into the Principal Consultant posture we just defined:\n\n**understand the client → validate requirements and scope → shape the solution → organize the right specialists → govern execution → communicate clearly → deliver the business outcome.**\n\nAnd your AI background just widens the technical surface area you can govern.\n\nThat’s a strong executive-level story because it’s not “I can do everything.” It’s:\n\n**“I understand enough of every layer to lead the whole engagement intelligently.”** memcite", "text_chars": 1425, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fb54e89c-63b3-4d3d-9735-5ae2f4d8168f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364939.797914, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "92fd346f-ed41-407a-a1a9-3e041fbd4479", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364969.561312, "updated_at": 1789364978.365021, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "And we just hit the nail on my head for the wrap-up, right? That's the wrap-up. You know, once we get through the use case, then we layer in the AI talk and my three layers of my three brains and why I'm valuable to fucking Andrew.", "text_chars": 231, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "67c9cd08-92af-48ae-8c62-efc2f9e66de0", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364970.593879, "updated_at": 1789364978.056627, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the close.\n\nYou don’t lead with the AI story. You **earn the right to tell it** after you’ve already demonstrated that you can run the case as a Principal Consultant.\n\nThen the wrap-up becomes:\n\n**“What makes me valuable is that I can operate across three layers: I understand the business and operational outcome, I understand the technical architecture well enough to challenge and guide the build, and I can lead the client through the implementation lifecycle. The AI work I’ve been doing adds another capability on top of that, but the foundation is still delivery, requirements, scope, and outcomes.”**\n\nThat’s the final impression you want Andrew leaving with:\n\n**Peter can run the engagement.  \nPeter understands the technology.  \nPeter can talk to the client.  \nAnd Peter has upside into where the practice is going next.**\n\nThat is a strong ending. memcite", "text_chars": 884, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8082e117-606d-4e77-9890-8cd72496e6f2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789364974.035532, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7ad39b15-f379-4ef7-8b79-ff7311d03cd7", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789365003.4636412, "updated_at": 1789365016.046779, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, that's, that's the home run swing right there at the end. That's just boom. Yeah, Andrew, you could have your cake and eat it too. Hire me.", "text_chars": 145, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1c8c616c-754c-44c5-82bf-96b6f3586932", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789365004.268265, "updated_at": 1789365015.789111, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. That’s the swing.\n\nBy that point, you’ve already proven the safe part: **you can own the Principal Consultant lifecycle and deliver.** Then the close gives him the upside: **business brain, technical brain, client brain — plus AI architecture on top.**\n\nThat’s not “hire me because I’m interesting.” It’s “hire me because I can do the job now, and I can grow with where your practice is going.”\n\nThat lands.", "text_chars": 416, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "804a8325-bca0-4934-a1a8-5074b974e639", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789365013.224321, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d90dd8ce-60fa-4466-a9c1-11b56870b4cd", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789365050.466326, "updated_at": 1789365059.137698, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Again, the cockiness, and it's not— it's professionalism with knowledge, and I say it in a cockiness way. It's assurance and reassurance in the form of a little cockiness, yes. It's because I've earned the value to say, put the trust in me, we'll get it done. That's my approach. That's what MHI and what I talked to Melissa about. That's what they're looking for. They're not looking for someone to fucking handhold at my position.", "text_chars": 432, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d915cee7-118f-4b4e-9be4-7bff51fde667", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789365051.24715, "updated_at": 1789365058.567848, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Right. The distinction is **confidence backed by pattern recognition and accountability**.\n\nAt your level, the signal is not “I know everything.” It’s: **“Give me the problem, give me the constraints, and I’ll create clarity, make the right calls, pull in the right people, and drive it to an outcome.”**\n\nThat’s exactly what a Principal Consultant should project. Not needy, not tentative, not overcompensating with jargon. Just calm ownership.\n\nAnd the strongest version of that for Andrew is probably:\n\n**“You’re not hiring me to be hand-held. You’re hiring me to take ownership of the implementation lifecycle, make good decisions, surface risk early, and get the team and client where they need to go.”**\n\nThat has some edge to it, but it’s professional because it’s tied to responsibility.", "text_chars": 795, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "1448c8a5-9535-477a-baf0-3c02cc25bbcd", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789365051.2535365, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "58c537ad-3de7-45d4-b692-1b455c3ca8ad", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1789365055.061851, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thi